    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate focusing on high-floor utility and aggressive stolen base potential. **Seiya Suzuki** leads our lineup with a 75.03 efficiency score, benefiting significantly from a favorable matchup against a left-handed starter and a slow delivery that projects an elite 90% steal probability for his base-running profile. **Corbin Carroll** remains a core anchor at 70.87; while he faces a left-handed pitcher, his ability to manufacture speed-based production keeps him locked in despite the minor platoon regression. **Brice Turang** and **Mickey Moniak** also provide strong value today at 69.94 and 69.13 respectively, with both players gaining significant boosts from advantageous matchups against Stock and Waldron, allowing them to provide consistent production across the 5x5 categories.\n\nThe optimization process was tightest regarding **Austin Riley**, who sits at 54.66 and narrowly maintained his UTIL spot. While his raw power is high, his position in the #8 hole suppressed his scoring potential compared to middle-of-the-order threats. We are closely monitoring the status of **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Max Muncy**, and **Gabriel Moreno**, as the bench remains crowded; however, none could displace our starters given the current platoon-favorable alignments. We also continue to monitor the IL reports for **James Wood**, **Trevor Story**, and **Giancarlo Stanton**, whose absence remains a significant drain on our categorical ceiling. **Vinnie Pasquantino** remains a \"marginal\" start at 52.85; while he is currently active, his score is tempered by a difficult matchup against Miles, and he is a candidate for early removal if our game cap efficiency necessitates a pivot to depth pieces like **Dalton Rushing**, **Edwin Arroyo**, **Cole Young**, or **Henry Bolte**.",
